---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';

const path = '/for/beauty';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'for/beauty');
const desc = description(site, 'for/beauty');
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a href="/for" class="link text-sm font-bold">← All store types</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Choosing Shopify cart recovery apps for beauty brands</h1>
    <div class="prose-shopify mt-7">
      <h2>Choose around the unresolved product decision</h2>
      <p>A beauty cart may contain a specific shade, a routine bundle or a subscription option. Choose recovery software by whether it preserves that decision and helps answer the remaining question. A generic best-app ranking says little about whether a recovered link restores the intended variant or selling plan.</p>
      <h2>Keep checkout recovery and replenishment separate</h2>
      <p>Shopify distinguishes browse, cart and checkout recovery. Start with the native automation baseline and inspect which event your store actually produces. Replenishment is a later purchase-cycle decision: it should use product usage and purchase context rather than treating every unconverted cart as an empty bottle. Do not assume a universal 30–90 day window across sample sizes, routines and household usage.</p>
      <h2>Resolve shade and routine uncertainty</h2>
      <p>For shade-sensitive products, include the selected variant and a route to the store’s shade help. If that shade is unavailable, suppress stale urgency and offer an appropriate stock notification path where available. For bundles, show the actual contents and explain the intended routine using approved product information. Avoid inventing suitability or treatment claims to fill a recovery message.</p>
      <h2>Evaluate candidates using your catalog</h2>
      <p>Sequenzy, Klaviyo and Omnisend can be candidates to investigate alongside native Shopify automation; this page does not certify their integrations or rank their performance. Ask each candidate to show variant restoration, stock-aware messaging, purchase exits and any subscription data connection your store needs. Obtain the actual plan and usage quote for your audience and channels before comparing total costs.</p>
      <h2>Proposed acceptance exercise: shade plus selling plan</h2>
      <p>Create a test cart with a specific shade and subscription option, then inspect the recovered checkout. Does it retain the variant, quantity, purchase option and disclosed terms? Repeat after the shade sells out and after the buyer completes a one-time purchase elsewhere in the store. Check which messages stop and whether subscription and one-time audiences remain distinguishable. This is a proposed exercise, not a report of testing.</p>
      <h2>Prevent conflicting offers</h2>
      <p>List welcome codes, bundle savings, subscription incentives and recovery discounts in one offer map. Check eligibility and combination behavior in checkout before activating a reminder. Hypothetical example: an $80 basket with $36 product cost produces $44 gross profit. A 15% discount costs $12 and leaves $32 gross profit before fulfillment, payment fees and returns. Include samples and shipping subsidies in the contribution decision; they still have costs.</p>
      <h2>Set timing around the question</h2>
      <p>A first reminder can restore the cart and offer product help; a later message should address a real remaining obstacle such as delivery or returns. Select delays from your buying cycle and support availability rather than copying minute-level defaults. Stop recovery after purchase, suppress unavailable items and coordinate any eligible SMS with email so a buyer is not receiving the same offer from multiple flows.</p>
      <h2>Migrate and measure by purchase type</h2>
      <p>Before a switch, record current flow ownership, active codes, permission states and subscription exclusions. Disable duplicate recovery entry points and use fresh eligible events rather than replaying old carts. Compare completed orders and contribution by one-time versus subscription purchase and by offer exposure. Include cancellations and refunds in the observation window. A proposed holdout needs enough volume and a defined assignment method before it can support an incremental-lift claim.</p>
      <h2>Primary references and scope</h2>
      <p><a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/marketing-automations/create">Shopify’s marketing automation guide</a> describes native recovery stages. Supplier-specific feature availability remains an account-level evaluation.</p>
      <p>Related: <a href="/compare">app comparisons</a> and <a href="/for">other store types</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
